    hook: "The ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle identified three modes of persuasion over 2,000 years ago — and they still describe every argument you encounter today. Logos appeals to logic. Ethos appeals to credibility. Pathos appeals to emotion. Every speech, essay, advertisement, and debate uses some combination of these three. Understanding them gives you X-ray vision into how arguments work. Quill the Porcupine evaluates every argument through Aristotle's timeless framework.",
    bigIdea: "Aristotle's three appeals — logos (logic and evidence), ethos (credibility and character), and pathos (emotion) — are the foundation of all persuasion, and analyzing which appeals an author uses reveals how they are trying to convince you.",
    sections: [
      { title: "Logos: The Appeal to Logic", emoji: "🧠", content: "Logos persuades through reasoning and evidence — facts, statistics, logical arguments, and cause-and-effect reasoning. A logos-heavy argument might say: studies show that students who read 20 minutes daily score 15% higher on standardized tests. The strength of logos is that it appeals to the rational mind. Its weakness is that logic alone can feel cold and impersonal, so most effective arguments combine logos with other appeals." },
      { title: "Ethos: The Appeal to Credibility", emoji: "🏛️", content: "Ethos persuades by establishing the speaker's credibility, expertise, and trustworthiness. When a doctor recommends exercise, their medical degree is ethos. When an author cites their 20 years of research, that is ethos. Ethos also comes from tone — a fair, respectful, knowledgeable tone builds ethos, while an arrogant or dismissive tone destroys it. Audiences trust speakers they perceive as qualified, honest, and fair." },
      { title: "Pathos: The Appeal to Emotion", emoji: "❤️", content: "Pathos persuades by making the audience feel something — sympathy, anger, joy, fear, hope, or guilt. A charity ad showing a hungry child uses pathos. A speech about freedom that makes your heart swell uses pathos. Pathos is powerful because emotions drive action more than logic alone. But pathos without logos can be manipulative — strong arguments use emotion to support logic, not replace it." },
      { title: "Analyzing the Balance", emoji: "⚖️", content: "Most effective arguments use all three appeals. A speech might open with a personal story (pathos), establish the speaker's expertise (ethos), and present data and reasoning (logos). The balance depends on the audience and purpose. An academic paper is heavy on logos. A fundraising speech is heavy on pathos. A recommendation from a trusted leader relies on ethos. Quill evaluates which appeals dominate and whether the balance is appropriate." }
    ],
