    title: "Media Analysis: How Messages Are Constructed", duration: "15-20 min",
    hook: "Every image in a magazine is carefully selected, edited, and placed. Every word in a headline is chosen for maximum impact. Every scene in a movie is crafted to make you feel a specific emotion. Media does not just reflect reality — it constructs a version of reality designed to achieve a purpose. Understanding how media messages are constructed gives you the power to engage with them consciously rather than being shaped by them unknowingly. Quill the Porcupine deconstructs every message because he values his own judgment.",
    bigIdea: "Media analysis examines how messages are constructed through selection, framing, and technique — understanding these construction choices empowers you to interpret media critically rather than passively absorb it.",
    sections: [
      { title: "Selection and Omission", emoji: "✂️", content: "Every media message involves choices about what to include and what to leave out. A news photograph shows one moment from one angle — countless other moments and angles were not chosen. A headline emphasizes one aspect of a complex story. An advertisement shows the product's benefits but not its limitations. What is selected shapes your perception. What is omitted shapes it equally. Critical media analysis asks both: what am I seeing, and what am I not seeing?" },
      { title: "Framing", emoji: "🖼️", content: "Framing is how the same information is presented to create different impressions. The glass is half full versus the glass is half empty — same fact, different frame. A news story about a protest can be framed as a movement for justice or a threat to public order depending on the words, images, and sources chosen. Framing does not change the facts — it changes how you feel about the facts. Recognizing framing is one of the most important media analysis skills." },
      { title: "Visual and Audio Techniques", emoji: "🎬", content: "Media uses visual and audio techniques to create emotional responses. Camera angles (looking up at a person makes them seem powerful; looking down makes them seem small), lighting (bright suggests safety; shadows suggest danger), music (minor keys create tension; major keys create joy), and editing pace (fast cuts create excitement; slow cuts create contemplation) all manipulate your emotional experience without changing the content." },
      { title: "Representation and Stereotypes", emoji: "👥", content: "Media shapes how groups of people are perceived. When certain groups are consistently shown in limited roles — villains, victims, sidekicks — it reinforces stereotypes. When diverse and complex representation appears, it challenges them. Critical media analysis asks: whose stories are being told? Who is represented and how? Whose perspectives are missing? How might this representation affect how people see themselves and others?" }
    ],
    activity: { title: "Media Deconstruction", instructions: "Choose one media message to deconstruct completely: a magazine ad, a news article with photos, a movie trailer, or a social media campaign. Analyze: what was selected and what was likely omitted? How is the subject framed — and how could it be framed differently? What visual or audio techniques create emotional effects? Who is represented and how? What is the intended audience and purpose? Write a full analysis (one page) that reveals the construction behind the message. Compare your analysis with someone who saw the same message — did they notice the same techniques?" },
    quiz: [
      { question: "What does it mean that media constructs reality?", answer: "Media does not just reflect the world — it creates a version of reality through selective choices about what to include, exclude, and emphasize", options: ["Media builds physical structures", "Media does not just reflect the world — it creates a version of reality through selective choices about what to include, exclude, and emphasize", "Everything in media is fictional", "Media always shows objective truth"] },
      { question: "What is framing in media?", answer: "How the same information is presented to create different impressions — like half full vs half empty", options: ["Putting a picture in a frame", "How the same information is presented to create different impressions — like half full vs half empty", "The border around a TV screen", "Adding a caption to a photograph"] },
      { question: "How do camera angles affect perception?", answer: "Looking up makes a subject seem powerful; looking down makes them seem small or vulnerable", options: ["Camera angles have no effect on perception", "Looking up makes a subject seem powerful; looking down makes them seem small or vulnerable", "Only professional photographers understand angles", "Angles only matter in movies, not in news"] },
      { question: "Why does media representation matter?", answer: "How groups are shown in media shapes how people perceive those groups — and how members of those groups see themselves", options: ["It does not matter — media is just entertainment", "How groups are shown in media shapes how people perceive those groups — and how members of those groups see themselves", "Only children are affected by representation", "Representation only matters in news media"] },
      { question: "What is the first question to ask when analyzing any media message?", answer: "What choices were made — what was selected, omitted, and emphasized — and why?", options: ["Is this entertaining?", "What choices were made — what was selected, omitted, and emphasized — and why?", "How much did it cost to produce?", "Is this available for free?"] }
    ],
    familyAdventure: "Choose a news story covered by two different outlets. Compare coverage side by side: what images does each use? What quotes do they highlight? How do their headlines frame the story differently? What does each one omit that the other includes? Discuss as a family: did reading both change your understanding of the story? This exercise shows that no single media source tells the complete story — and that media-literate citizens seek multiple perspectives.",
